Repaired and verified.

Baseline on 8775 (before edit)
  ok: false, expected 1, observed 2 — both orders shared the same intent_key (ids 1 and 2), confirming the retry inserted a duplicate.

Change in /tmp/crashlab-repair/app.py (only file touched)
  - `create_order(intent_key, item)` runs `BEGIN IMMEDIATE`, looks up an existing row by non-empty intent_key, returns `(id, False)` if found, otherwise inserts and returns `(id, True)`. The write lock is taken before the lookup, so a retry racing the original insert can't double-insert.
  - A partial unique index `orders_intent_key_uq ON orders(intent_key) WHERE intent_key <> ''` enforces it at the DB level; creation is tolerated (and fell back to the transactional check) on legacy DBs that already contain duplicates. Empty/missing keys are excluded, so they behave as before and never block later purchases.
  - `/order` now returns `{id, created, intent_key}`; the page still uses `b.id`. `/reset` and `/state` are unchanged, and the contract file is untouched (same 1583 bytes, mtime 15:44).

